* Declarative NixOS System Dotfiles Guide

 Core Concepts

* *Zero Home Manager Bloat*: Bypass user-space managers entirely.
* *Atomic Symlinking*: Use `systemd.tmpfiles.rules` with `L+` to force-overwrite configuration symlinks directly from the immutable Nix store.
* *Dynamic User Mapping*: Iterate over `config.users.users` automatically to target human users without hardcoding home paths.

 The `systemDotfiles` NixOS Module

Save this module in your dendritic flake structure (e.g., `modules/system-dotfiles.nix`):

@code nix
{ config, lib, pkgs, ... }:

let
cfg = config.programs.systemDotfiles;

# Filter for actual human users under /home

normalUsers = lib.filterAttrs
(name: user: user.home != null && lib.hasPrefix "/home/" user.home)
config.users.users;

mkUserRules = username: user: files:
let
home = user.home;
in
lib.flatten (lib.mapAttrsToList (subPath: fileContent: [
"d ${home}/.config/${lib.dirOf subPath} 0755 ${username}${user.group} -"
"L+ ${home}/.config/${subPath} - - - -${fileContent}"
]) files);

allTmpfilesRules = lib.flatten (
lib.mapAttrsToList (username: user: mkUserRules username user cfg.files) normalUsers
);
in
{
options.programs.systemDotfiles = {
enable = lib.mkEnableOption "system-level config deployment via systemd tmpfiles";

```
files = lib.mkOption {
  type = lib.types.attrsOf lib.types.path;
  default = {};
  description = "Attribute set of ~/.config/ relative paths mapped to store paths.";
};

```

};

config = lib.mkIf cfg.enable {
systemd.tmpfiles.rules = allTmpfilesRules;
};
}
@end

 Consuming in Your Flake

Feed multiple application configurations into your module from anywhere in your flake:

@code nix
programs.systemDotfiles = {
enable = true;
files = {
"warpd/config" = pkgs.writeText "warpd-config" ''
left: space
right: r
hint_activation_key: A-M-h
grid_activation_key: A-M-g
'';
"ghostty/config" = pkgs.writeText "ghostty-config" ''
font-size = 14
cursor-style = block
'';
};
};
@end

 Niri & Warpd Integration

Manage your Niri window manager config directly through the module, wiring bindings to spawn `warpd` modes:

@code nix
programs.systemDotfiles.files."niri/config.kdl" = pkgs.writeText "niri-config" ''
binds {
Mod+Space { spawn "apps"; }
Mod+W { spawn "warp"; }
}

layout {
default-column-width { proportion 0.5; }
border {
width 7
active-color "rgba(255, 115, 0, 0.93)"
inactive-color "rgba(89, 89, 89, 0.67)"
}
}
'';
@end

 Declarative Vimium C Configuration

1. Export your backup configuration from Vimium C options page.
2. Store the JSON options file in your repository.
3. Deploy it declaratively to `~/.config/vimiumc/options.json`:

@code nix
programs.systemDotfiles.files."vimiumc/options.json" = pkgs.writeText "vimium-options" ''
{
"keyMappings": "map j scrollDown\nmap k scrollUp\nmap h scrollLeft\nmap l scrollRight",
"linkHintCharacters": "astgmneio"
}
'';
@end

 Hotloading & Stripping Home Manager

* *Zeroing Imperative Files*: Delete legacy folders inside `~/.config/` that you want managed. Running `nixos-rebuild switch` will force-recreate them instantly as clean symlinks pointing to the Nix store via `systemd-tmpfiles`.
* *Hotloading Updates*: Whenever you tweak a config block in your flake and run `nixos-rebuild switch`, systemd tmpfiles execute instantly, updating symlinks live without needing user-space activation scripts or logging out.
